`lib/Git/Libgit2/FFI.pm` binds two function groups that no test in `t/` ever calls:

    grep -rn "git_graph_ahead_behind\|git_graph_descendant_of\|git_clone" t/ | wc -l
    0

GRAPH (`FFI.pm:1898`):
- `git_graph_ahead_behind(\my $ahead, \my $behind, $repo, $local, $upstream)` - counts
  the commits two commits are ahead/behind of each other
- `git_graph_descendant_of($repo, $commit, $ancestor)` - is `$commit` a descendant of
  `$ancestor`

Both take a `git_graph` handle that `git_graph_new`/`git_graph_free` create - and those
two are bound as well, but untested too.

CLONE (`FFI.pm:1345`):
- `git_clone_options_init($opts_ptr, $version)`
- `git_clone(\my $repo, $url, $path, $opts)`

A clone needs no network: `t/18-remote-local.t` already builds a local remote, so
`git_clone` against a path is testable the same way.

What a test would have to be careful about: `git_graph_new` takes the repository, and
both `git_graph_*` calls need the graph freed again. The repository handle in the tests
is created per test file and freed at the end - the graph has to be freed before it.

Done when: a test file covers the GRAPH group against a repository with a known
divergence (ahead/behind counts and the descendant relation both asserted), and the
CLONE group against a local remote, with `git_clone_options_init` initialising the
options struct.

